{
  "image_name": "da93995a3ea0c7adaf6ba15838c6c3a7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how near-eye displays work in virtual-reality and augmented-reality devices, and then contrasts three common microdisplay technologies used inside them.\n\nAt the top, VR and AR are shown as different optical setups. In a VR headset, light from microdisplays passes through lenses into an enclosed viewing space, so the user sees only the generated image in a dark, isolated environment. In AR glasses, a microdisplay sends light through a lens and an optical combiner, which mixes the projected image with incoming light from the real world. The arrows emphasize that AR operates in a bright environment, where digital content is overlaid on what the user already sees.\n\nThe lower part focuses on display architectures. LCoS is depicted as a reflective system: incident light enters through a polarizer, interacts with liquid crystal on a silicon backplane and reflective layer, and then exits as reflected light. OLED-on-silicon is shown as an emissive stack in which a white OLED and colored filters generate the output image. LED-on-silicon is also emissive, but uses direct RGB microLED emitters instead of white light plus filters. Overall, the figure highlights the different optical demands of VR versus AR and the key structural differences between reflective and emissive microdisplays.",
